import{_ as a,P as i,d as n,e as o,x as r,q as s,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.9 HV09 Structural Load (Full Interface Card)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.9 HV09 Structural Load (Full Interface Card)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1543,"xkLessons":{"A.9":{"title":"A.9 HV09 Structural Load (Complete Interface Card)","lead":"Fewer tasks do not mean costs have disappeared, and being able to bear a burden does not mean one ought to. HV09 first examines tasks, capacity, recovery, and specific cost-bearing positions together.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4182–4247; original site location /theory/appendix-a/a-09#section-a-9. For the current full text, see the theory-v2 source; historical research manuscripts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: “High efficiency or high carrying capacity does not constitute justification.” HV09's object is not abstract entropy but the relation of task and coordination demands to capacity and recovery margins under mappings of specific windows, positions, and types. Tasks, coordination losses, maintenance, capacity, recovery, and cost-bearing positions must be registered together; being able to withstand a burden does not mean one should continue to bear it. [T-main]","HV09-R0-instant-task-capacity provides only same-window descriptions, without requiring G2 or CM-LOAD to hold beforehand. Tasks, margins, backlogs, local gaps, and recovery states may be recorded, but one person's shortfall today cannot independently establish damage, an overload mechanism, or inevitable collapse. Same-window data require consistent units and preservation of the distribution of cost-bearing positions. [T-main]","HV09-R1-overload-mechanism requires complete applicability of CM-LOAD and qualified G2 identification of an above-threshold effect of load, replenishment, load-reduction, or recovery channels on the preselected outcome, with CAUSAL/E4 added. It then permits mechanism candidates bounded by position, type, and window. No channel is generalized into an entropy law for all positions, nor is an obligation created concerning who must reduce load or make sacrifices. [T-main; T-load]","HV09-R2-cumulative-overload requires G3 in addition to G2 and CM-LOAD: historical load must have a conditional increment for subsequent capacity, errors, or recovery beyond the current representation. HV09-R3-carried-cumulative-overload instead checks current damage or capacity carriers, formation, retention or recovery, and discriminating cross-window causal material under §4.5. G2 covers the outcomes in the claimed window, without registering G3 qualification. Neither route holds merely because years of busyness are observed. [T-main; T-history]","Once a momentary gap is resolved, no lasting difference may remain; capacity can also expand alongside demand and absorb it. Accumulation is not necessarily monotonic. H5 traces support only trace candidates and cannot replace later effects. Failure to support the framework's formal route also cannot erase general harms, persistent burdens, or historical causality supported by other independent domain evidence; each remains on its own grounds. [T-main; T-load]","Execution, simultaneous activity, management, standby, and opportunity costs should have separate accounts according to the question. Clock minutes of parallel care and household work overlap and cannot simply be added into a single work-time total. Standby restrictions can be real without every minute being active execution. A transfer across positions can reduce tasks in the original post without reducing costs for households or outsourced positions. Statistical averages can hide critical bottlenecks. [T-main]","States of low load, manageable load, criticality, overload, and recovery require window-specific evidence; they are not character ratings. Absence may be protective and cannot be treated as laziness. Exit and recovery curves, invisible labor, and outsourcing costs should enter uncertainty and exclusion areas. The nine axes particularly preserve load-capacity distributions and different J authorities. [T-main]","This card outputs load-capacity descriptions and needs for load reduction or additional resources; it does not authorize performance-related action, compelled burden-bearing, or task adjustments. Those taking up work should be able to report safely and trigger independent review. Authorized rollback must not only remove erroneous labels but also address performance and responsibility effects, actual tasks and resources, and records, with completion verified. [T-main]","The design for three US states in the 2005 Cash and Counseling report separates tasks of counselors, participants, and fiscal services. It helps identify who may bear management and payment labor; it does not mean this discussion measured total burdens or established a recovery mechanism. Two pages of the previously available original PDF were read; the current official website's 403 status is explicitly retained. [P-CASH]","Field-by-field study of all 39 fields (“Original card” below retains the field’s complete content in translation; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Interface ID (id). Original card, translated from Chinese: HV09. Interpretation: This is the short identifier within the card collection, tracking the same interface; it does not mean a real object has been proved. [T-main]","02 | Qualified ID (qualified_id). Original card, translated from Chinese: human_variable:HV09. Interpretation: A qualified namespace avoids name collisions; references to this ID must still carry the object and evidence status. [T-main]","03 | Name (name). Original card, translated from Chinese: Structural load. Interpretation: The name provides an entry point for reading, not an automatic label for a particular person, group, or real structure. [T-main]","04 | Claim type (claim_type). Original card: H. Interpretation: H identifies the role of a human-instance/interface proposition; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Contract role (contract_role). Original card: human_variable_interface. Interpretation: this variable-interface contract specifies how to enter analysis; a field’s existence does not mean an empirical instance has been established. [T-main]","06 | Proposition (proposition). Original card, translated from Chinese: Human structural load must register tasks, coordination losses, maintenance requirements, capacity, recovery margins, and cost-bearing positions together. Interpretation: Read this proposition together with the conditional routes and ceilings below; a single sentence cannot yield an unconditional causal or normative conclusion. [T-main]","07 | Scope (scope). Original card, translated from Chinese: Human structures under sustained operation, maintenance, care, or high pressure. Interpretation: The scope lists task domains that may be examined; it does not mean every object within them has empirical support. [T-main]","08 | Pause condition (pause_condition). Original card, translated from Chinese: Only entropy, vulnerability, or resilience metaphors are used, without tasks, capacity, and recovery mechanisms. Interpretation: Pausing targets judgments or adoption relying on the premise; independently established descriptions may remain, but invalidated premises cannot continue to be used. [T-main]","09 | Inferential dependencies (inferential_requires). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: Inferential dependencies provide the upstream content needed to establish a proposition. Upstream candidate/supported status must be carried through unchanged; citation does not automatically upgrade it. [T-main]","10 | Protocol dependencies (protocol_requires). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. EVIDENCE; 2. SOURCE. Interpretation: Protocol dependencies constrain evidence collection and expression; completing protocols does not independently establish the truth of empirical propositions. [T-main]","11 | Qualification / specialization (specializes). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: an empty set means only that no such specialization relations are registered, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a nonempty set also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Applicable-object references (applies_to). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: applicable-object references cannot replace the current object contract; an empty set is explicit registration, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Conditional-support routes (conditional_support_routes). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. route_id=HV09-R0-instant-task-capacity;; claim_level=descriptive_classification;; when=Task or coordination demands, capacity, recovery margins, and their distributions are observable within the same window, position, and type mapping.;; additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);; additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);; allowed_conclusion=Register instantaneous task–capacity relations, margins, backlogs, local gaps, and recovery states.;; result_ceiling=Same-window description only; momentary peaks or gaps do not automatically become overload mechanisms, cumulative damage, or collapse.; 2. route_id=HV09-R1-overload-mechanism;; claim_level=mechanism_explanation;; when=CM-LOAD's applicability conditions are complete and a qualified G2-instance shows an above-threshold effect of load, replenishment, load-reduction, or recovery channels on the preselected outcome.;; additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance, CM-LOAD;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register candidate overload or recovery mechanisms within specified positions, types, windows, and channels.;; result_ceiling=Do not generalize into entropy, resilience, or inevitable collapse at every position, or directly generate obligations of load reduction or sacrifice.; 3. route_id=HV09-R2-cumulative-overload;; claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;; when=The overload mechanism already has G2-instance and CM-LOAD support, and a G3-instance shows a conditional increment of historical load for subsequent capacity, errors, or recovery.;; additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance, CM-LOAD, G3-instance;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register candidate cumulative damage or delayed recovery of the historical-increment type beyond the current state, within preregistered carriers, windows, and outcomes.;; result_ceiling=Do not infer irreversibility, inevitable collapse, attribution of responsibility, or obligations for named actors to bear burdens.; 4. route_id=HV09-R3-carried-cumulative-overload;; claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;; when=CM-LOAD-carried-cumulative conditions are complete, a qualified G2-instance covers the specified load, replenishment, or recovery channels and claimed cross-window outcomes, and current damage or capacity carriers, cross-window accumulation or recovery, and discriminating causal material are verified under §4.5.;; additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance, CM-LOAD;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register candidate carrier-transmitted cumulative damage or delayed recovery within specified carriers, windows, and outcomes.;; result_ceiling=Do not register a G3 historical increment, infer a damage mechanism from a single overload or continuing correlation, or infer irreversibility, inevitable collapse, responsibility, or obligations of sacrifice. Interpretation: R0 is same-window description; R1 is mechanism; R2 is a historical increment beyond the current representation; R3 is accumulation in current carriers. Routes are not severity levels. [T-main]","14 | Allowed inference (allowed_inference). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Candidate overload, insufficient margins, maintenance gaps, and recovery differences. Interpretation: These are permitted directions of conclusion; which is actually reached, and at what strength, remains bounded by the selected route and material. [T-main]","15 | Prohibited leap (prohibited_leap). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Those taking up work should continue bearing it; 2. High load proves devotion; 3. An overloaded actor is an mechanism of destabilization. Interpretation: These are common boundary crossings that cannot be avoided merely by rephrasing; examine actual inferences and subsequent use, not just wording. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale profile (scale_profile). Original card, translated from Chinese: SP=<A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J>; A=Aggregation level: individual task loads, uptake cases, post or group populations, load-capacity distributions, and aggregation rules; X=Spatial scope: work or care sites, organizational boundaries, digital-labor spaces, and cross-domain outsourcing scope; T=Temporal span: momentary peaks, persistent backlogs, recovery delays, and cross-period and intergenerational windows; O=Organizational level: uptake roles, teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies; C=Causal level: task events, task–capacity interaction mechanisms, meso-level bottleneck structures, institutional distribution, and system conditions; R=Observation resolution: raw task and work-time records, load sequences, uptake cases, capacity distributions, delay and error indicators, and summaries, with compression losses registered; I=Range of impact: those directly taking up work, people dependent on services, indirect substitutes, second-order spillovers, and cross-domain and intergenerational costs; N=Network-topology scope: task dependencies, critical bottlenecks, substitute nodes, recovery paths, and cross-domain outsourcing networks; J=Jurisdiction and authorization scope: separately register authorization for task allocation, resource adjustments, stopping, performance-related use, and remedies; preserve load-capacity distributions. Interpretation: Register the nine axes item by item. In particular, range of impact I creates no jurisdictional authority J; when observation resolution R decreases, preserve compression losses. [T-main]","17 | Effective object (effective_object). Original card, translated from Chinese: The structural relation of task and coordination demands to available capacity and recovery margins within a given window. Interpretation: This describes the object corresponding to the interface; choosing a low-threshold descriptive route does not permit treating functional meaning in the name as proven causality. [T-main]","18 | Cross-scale invariants (scale_invariants). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Load, capacity, recovery, and cost-bearing positions. Interpretation: Invariants are comparative responsibilities to preserve during transfer, not a claim that variable values are unchanged at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Required additions for elevation (required_scale_additions). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Load distribution; 2. Concealment through aggregation; 3. Inheritance of responsibility; 4. Intergenerational impacts. Interpretation: Elevation is not copying and enlarging the original table. When material about new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and other items is incomplete, limit conclusions that depend on them. [T-main]","20 | Semantics changing with scale (changing_semantics). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Bottlenecks, capacity, and modes of recovery may change across levels. Interpretation: A word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; rebuild the correspondence rather than assume the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Non-applicable objects (non_applicable_objects). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Processes without sustained nonequilibrium, maintenance, or human uptake requirements. Interpretation: Non-applicability constraints prevent unconditional projection of interfaces concerning human norms, responsibility, and experience; they do not deny that other theories may study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Forbidden elevation (forbidden_elevation). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Average load concealing local overload. Interpretation: Inferences from local parts to wholes need separate proof; broad impact, grand names, and formal similarity cannot supply representativeness or authorization. [T-main]","23 | State set (state). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Low load; 2. Manageable; 3. Critical; 4. Overloaded; 5. Recovering. Interpretation: States are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not maturity ladders for scoring people; state changes require windows, grounds, and versions. [T-main]","24 | Observables (observables). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Task volume per unit time, backlogs, delays, and error rates; 2. Personnel and resource capacity, invisible labor, and availability of substitutes; 3. Stopping, absence, exit, and recovery curves; 4. Distributional differences between average load and critical local uptake positions. Interpretation: Observable clues indicate where to find material; a single clue's existence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements (evidence). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Task volume; 2. Delays and errors; 3. Personnel and resources; 4. Recovery records; 5. Exit and absence; 6. Distinguish execution, simultaneous activity, management, standby, and opportunity costs according to the objective, explaining overlap, units, and transfers across positions rather than directly adding them into a unified burden; 7. When the task in question depends on uninterrupted periods, separately record how default-contact, standby, and temporary gap-filling responsibilities fragment available continuous blocks, without converting this into hours. Interpretation: Distinguish execution, parallel activity, management, standby, and opportunity costs according to the objective; explain units and overlaps before calculating, without inventing a unified burden quantity. [T-main]","26 | Input dependencies and interface content (input_dependencies). Original card: 1. Uptake layer; 2. Drive–uptake chain; 3. Conditional potential field; 4. R0 registers task volume, capacity, backlog, and recovery of the same type, in the same time window and burden-bearing position, without making established G2 a prerequisite for description.; 5. R1 channel effects invoke G2 and CM-LOAD; R3 carrier-based accumulation checks the current state, formation, and cross-window effects under §4.5; R2's additional historical conditional increment is checked separately through G3. H5 traces do not replace subsequent effects or G3 testing.; 6. Lack of formal mechanism support under these routes does not cancel general harm, continuing burdens, or historical causation already supported by independent domain evidence; conclusions are registered separately on their own grounds. Interpretation: Insufficient formal-route support does not negate harm supported by independent domain evidence; H5 traces do not establish delayed recovery or G3. [T-main]","27 | Output effects and variable flows (output_effects). Original card: 1. State updates; 2. Behavior of destabilization; 3. Maintenance and repair needs. Interpretation: Variable flows indicate which downstream judgments may depend on this interface; passing references onward does not ensure downstream conclusions, actual repair, or authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag (time_window_and_lag). Original card: Distinguish immediate peaks, persistent backlogs, recovery lags, and intergenerational costs. Interpretation: Occurrence, observation, execution, and the arrival of effects may happen at different times; short-term material cannot automatically certify extension beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty (uncertainty). Original card: Record hidden labor, outsourced costs, and protective absence. Interpretation: Locate uncertainty in specific material and positions; do not merely write an overall confidence score that conceals positions never covered at all. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone (local_exclusion_zone). Original card: Informal labor, family care, outsourcing, and uptake positions with low visibility. Interpretation: An exclusion zone limits the coverage of the current inference; it does not erase these people or facts from the world. Material unavailable for safety reasons is neither consent nor evidence of no problem. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions (affected_positions). Original card: 1. Those taking up work; 2. Those dependent on services; 3. Substitutes; 4. Positions receiving cost spillovers. Interpretation: Register both directly and indirectly affected people; visible members do not represent all cost bearers. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carrier (carrier). Original card: 1. Personnel; 2. Posts; 3. Procedures; 4. Facilities; 5. Budgets. Interpretation: A carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something. Its name, completed construction, and delivery of the required function require different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible party (responsible_subject). Original card: 1. Task allocators; 2. Resource allocators; 3. Authorizers; 4. Supervisors; 5. Parties responsible for remedies. Interpretation: Specify who proposes a judgment and who operates or uses it; registering responsibility does not automatically constitute adjudication of legal liability or new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status (normative_status). Original card: High efficiency or high load-bearing capacity does not constitute normative justification. Interpretation: Distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status as specified by the card. Established facts do not independently determine value rankings, and normative reasons do not automatically establish empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling (judgment_ceiling). Original card: R0 describes tasks, capacity, and recovery within the same window; R1 forms a bounded channel explanation under G2 and CM-LOAD; R3 forms a judgment of cumulative damage or delayed recovery transmitted by current carriers under §4.5; R2 judges historical increments beyond the current representation only with additional G3. Persistent correlation, temporary overload, and overall averages do not replace the corresponding evidence. Interpretation: Keep four ceilings separate: same-window gaps, causal mechanisms, carrier accumulation, and historical conditional increments. Means or persistent correlations cannot justify moving to a higher level. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling (action_ceiling). Original card: This variable generates only descriptions of load, capacity, recovery, local overload, and needs for load reduction or additional resources. It does not authorize task reductions, resource investment, performance-related action, or forced burden-bearing; any real-world adjustment must separately pass C12, an explicit runtime N premise, J authorization, and O procedures. Interpretation: The interface itself does not confer real-world authority to act; actual actions require separate checks of choice, C12, explicit N, valid J, O, protection, and validity periods. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples (counterexamples). Original card: 1. Overall average capacity is sufficient, yet a few crucial uptake positions remain persistently overloaded; 2. Entropy or resilience metaphors are used without identifying tasks, capacity, and recovery channels. Interpretation: Use counterexamples to limit unconditional extrapolation. They may require narrowing the scope or obtaining new evidence, but do not automatically invalidate all bounded uses. [T-main]","38 | Appeal (appeal). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, those taking up work may report hidden labor, overload, and recovery needs through safe, accessible channels protecting against retaliation, and trigger review independent of the original load judgment, performance, or task decision chain. Interpretation: Appeals must be safely accessible, protected against retaliation, and capable of triggering independent review; a name alone does not imply authority to change errors. [T-main]","39 | Rollback (rollback). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, when rollback is authorized, the designated responsible person revokes erroneous load judgments and their performance or responsibility effects within the specified time limit, actually restores task, resource, and record states, and retains versions and verification of completion. Interpretation: Within valid authorization, actually revoke erroneous effects, correct downstream consequences, and verify completion; changing words in a document does not restore rights and resources to the person concerned. [T-main]","Evidence item 7 of the card is added only when the target depends on uninterrupted periods. Record how being the default contact, being on standby, and temporary gap-filling responsibilities divide usable uninterrupted stretches, retaining their lengths and predictability rather than converting them into an hours total. Execution, simultaneous activities, management, standby, and opportunity costs also require units and overlap to be marked; one large table cannot manufacture a unified burden through repeated addition."],"argumentSteps":["Fix units of the same type, burden-bearing positions, and windows; separate execution, parallel activities, management, standby, and opportunity costs.","R0 lists demand, capacity, margin, and backlog without first claiming damage.","R1 checks CM-LOAD applicability and G2 channel effects.","Cross-window R3 checks carriers, formation, recovery, and comparisons; R2 increments beyond the current representation separately require G3.","Check local positions, outsourcing, families, and protective absences concealed by averages.","Actual adjustments separately require N/J/O and C12; authorized rollback revokes performance and responsibility consequences and actual effects."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Same-window gap","leftText":"Describes demand exceeding available capacity.","rightTitle":"Damage mechanism","rightText":"Requires corresponding causal material about formation and subsequent outcomes."},{"leftTitle":"Execution hours","leftText":"Time actually occupied by tasks.","rightTitle":"Standby and opportunity costs","rightText":"Keep separate accounts according to the target; they cannot be directly added into a unified burden."},{"leftTitle":"Overall margin","leftText":"The aggregate may be positive.","rightTitle":"Local bottleneck","rightText":"This does not guarantee margins are interchangeable."},{"leftTitle":"High load-bearing capacity","leftText":"Empirically measured functional capacity.","rightTitle":"An obligation to continue bearing burdens","rightText":"This cannot follow directly from capacity."}],"boundaries":["Original-card counterexample: overall capacity is sufficient, but a few crucial uptake positions remain persistently overloaded.","Original-card counterexample: using entropy or resilience metaphors without identifying tasks, capacity, and recovery channels.","Teaching counterexample: clearing this week's backlog is used to claim that historical loads have absolutely no consequences, exceeding the current evidence.","Source boundary: temporary gaps and persistent correlations cannot replace evidence of cumulative damage."],"questions":["Can task gaps be registered without G2?","Why is R3 not the same as R2?","Can failure to pass the formal routes justify saying that harm does not exist?","Does high task intensity establish dedication and responsibility?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-9/figures/hv09-routes.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Management and fiscal tasks in the three-state personal-care program","href":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Management and fiscal tasks in the three-state personal-care program","text":"The 2005 report describes spending plans, employer-related counseling support, and fiscal payment services separately.","sourceLabel":"Effect of Consumer Direction on Adults’ Personal Care and Well-Being in Arkansas, New Jersey and Florida","sourceUrl":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf","sourceLocator":"Printed pages 7–8, PDF pages 19–20","boundary":"The design can help list tasks; it does not establish actual burdens or G2 recovery effects.","href":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.9","start_line":4275,"end_line":4340,"startLine":4275,"endLine":4340}],"outputId":"section-a-9","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: an average margin does not resolve a crucial gap","text":"Within a constructed window, post A has 6 hours of tasks of the same type and 4 hours of available capacity, while post B has 2 hours of tasks and 6 hours of capacity. Total tasks are 8 and total capacity 10, leaving an overall margin of 2. A still has a gap of 2, while B has a margin of 4. If qualifications and timing make the posts noninterchangeable, the overall margin cannot fill A's gap.","assumptions":"Task hours can be compared within the same post; noninterchangeable qualifications between the two posts are a fictional condition.","boundary":"This only illustrates an R0 distribution. It does not establish a G2 overload mechanism, damage, or responsibility on A's part."},{"title":"Teaching construction: parallel activity and standby cannot be double-counted as clock time","text":"A constructed record shows 40 minutes of care work within one hour, alongside 20 minutes of housework that entirely overlaps it. The combined direct activities occupy 40 minutes, not 60. A separate standby constraint lasting the whole hour must be listed separately, rather than added into a single 100-minute clock duration.","assumptions":"The complete overlap and all durations are fictional; the union of the activity sets is 40 minutes.","boundary":"This does not establish that housework or standby carries no burden. Other costs need corresponding measures; the union of clock intervals cannot replace experience."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: overall capacity is sufficient, but a few crucial uptake positions remain persistently overloaded.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: using entropy or resilience metaphors without identifying tasks, capacity, and recovery channels.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: clearing this week's backlog is used to claim that historical loads have absolutely no consequences, exceeding the current evidence.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: temporary gaps and persistent correlations cannot replace evidence of cumulative damage.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can task gaps be registered without G2?","answer":"Yes, through R0 within the same window, type, and position; a mechanism explanation requires separate evidence.","basis":"R0 and R1."},{"question":"Why is R3 not the same as R2?","answer":"R3 checks transmission by current carriers across windows; R2 additionally requires a historical conditional increment beyond the current representation.","basis":"Two distinct evidentiary responsibilities."},{"question":"Can failure to pass the formal routes justify saying that harm does not exist?","answer":"No. Register independent domain evidence on its own grounds.","basis":"Item 6 of input_dependencies."},{"question":"Does high task intensity establish dedication and responsibility?","answer":"No. Keep factual capacity, costs, and normative obligations separate.","basis":"Prohibited leaps and normative status."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-9/figures/hv09-routes.png","alt":"R0 same window; R1 mechanism; R2 historical increment; R3 accumulation in current carriers.","caption":"Based on the four routes in A.9, not stages of severity."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-9/figures/hv09-capacity.png","alt":"In the construction, A lacks 2 hours and B has 4 to spare; the overall margin of 2 does not automatically fill A's gap.","caption":"A teaching construction, not an empirical sample."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-9/figures/hv09-costs.png","alt":"Separate accounts for execution, parallel activity, management, standby, and opportunity costs; factual loads do not create obligations of sacrifice.","caption":"Based on the evidence and normative fields in A.9."}],"diagramBrief":"The three diagrams separately present the four routes, a constructed numerical example of local margins, and the units and permission boundaries of different costs.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["There is no actual G2/G3 or CM-LOAD instance; the numerical examples check only units and distributions.","Reading of the original PDF is limited to the two specified pages; program design is not treated as every participant's actual experience."],"taskId":"S-A-9","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.9, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4096–4161","claim_support":["The complete 39 fields, four routes, and evidence units of HV09."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-load","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§6.6, Chinese source lines 1368–1383","claim_support":["The instantaneous, carrier-accumulation, and historical-increment branches of CM-LOAD, and the noninevitability of damage."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-history","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.5, Chinese source lines 620–641","claim_support":["Current carriers and historical conditional increments beyond the current representation."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-CASH","title":"Effect of Consumer Direction on Adults’ Personal Care and Well-Being in Arkansas, New Jersey and Florida","url":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2005-05-16","locator":"Printed pages 7–8, PDF pages 19–20","claim_support":["In the program designs for Arkansas, New Jersey, and Florida in the United States, spending plans, counseling support, and fiscal services share different management tasks."],"limits":["The author read the two specified pages of the existing original PDF; retrieval from the current official website returned 403. Program design does not establish a reduction in total burdens, actual support received by all participants, or a formal G2 instance."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"A.9 HV09 Structural load (complete interface card); Chinese source lines 4182–4247; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-9/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-9/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-9/source/S-A-9.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-9/diagrams.md","editionNote":"The relevant explanation in this section has been revised for v2; the original research attachments and the scope of external-source review retain their historical status.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"b7a19fd1ad31526bf2dcfb725ed9b7312ded3381065bba9d0569f758adc43e65","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-09.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-09.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-09.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,m,g){const t=i("SectionLesson");return n(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-9" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.9</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV09 Structural Load (Full Interface Card) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-9" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.9 HV09 Structural Load (Full Interface Card) {#section-a-9}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A. Identity, Proposition, and Scope</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interface ID (<code>id</code>)</td><td>HV09</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified ID (<code>qualified_id</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable:HV09</code></td></tr><tr><td>Name (<code>name</code>)</td><td>Structural Load</td></tr><tr><td>Claim type (<code>claim_type</code>)</td><td>H</td></tr><tr><td>Contract role (<code>contract_role</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable_interface</code></td></tr><tr><td>Proposition (<code>proposition</code>)</td><td>Structural load in human structures must be recorded together with tasks, coordination losses, maintenance requirements, capacity, recovery margins, and positions bearing costs.</td></tr><tr><td>Scope (<code>scope</code>)</td><td>Human structures under conditions of continuing operation, maintenance, care, or high pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions for suspension (<code>pause_condition</code>)</td><td>Metaphors of entropy, fragility, or resilience are used without identifying tasks, capacity, and mechanisms of recovery</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>B. Formal Dependencies and Limits on Conclusions</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inferential dependencies (<code>inferential_requires</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol dependencies (<code>protocol_requires</code>)</td><td>1. EVIDENCE<br>2. SOURCE</td></tr><tr><td>Qualification/specialization (<code>specializes</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>References to applicable objects (<code>applies_to</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional support routes (<code>conditional_support_routes</code>)</td><td>1. route_id=HV09-R0-instant-task-capacity;<br>claim_level=descriptive_classification;<br>when=Tasks or coordination requirements, capacity, recovery margins, and their distributions are observable under the same window, position, and type mapping;<br>additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);<br>additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);<br>allowed_conclusion=Register the instantaneous task–capacity relation, margins, backlogs, local gaps, and recovery status;<br>result_ceiling=Description within the same window only. An instantaneous peak or gap does not automatically establish an overload mechanism, cumulative damage, or collapse.<br>2. route_id=HV09-R1-overload-mechanism;<br>claim_level=mechanism_explanation;<br>when=All conditions for applying CM-LOAD are met, and an eligible G2-instance shows an above-threshold effect of a channel for load, replenishment, load reduction, or recovery on the preselected outcome;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance, CM-LOAD;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate overload or recovery mechanism within the specified position, type, window, and channel;<br>result_ceiling=Do not generalize the result as entropy or resilience, or infer inevitable collapse at every position. It does not directly generate an obligation to reduce load or make sacrifices.<br>3. route_id=HV09-R2-cumulative-overload;<br>claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;<br>when=The overload mechanism already has support from a G2-instance and CM-LOAD, and a G3-instance shows that historical load provides a conditional increment for subsequent capacity, errors, or recovery;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance, CM-LOAD, G3-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate for cumulative damage or delayed recovery with a historical increment beyond the current state, limited to the pre-registered carrier, window, and outcome;<br>result_ceiling=Do not infer irreversibility, inevitable collapse, attribution of responsibility, or a duty for a named subject to bear the load.<br>4. route_id=HV09-R3-carried-cumulative-overload;<br>claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;<br>when=All conditions of CM-LOAD-carried-cumulative are met. An eligible G2-instance covers the specified channel of load, replenishment, or recovery and the stated cross-window outcome. Verify the carrier of current damage or capacity, accumulation or recovery across windows, and discriminating causal materials under §4.5;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance, CM-LOAD;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate for cumulative damage or delayed recovery through carrier transmission within the specified carrier, window, and outcome;<br>result_ceiling=Do not register a G3 historical increment or infer a damage mechanism from one overload event or a persistent correlation. Do not infer irreversibility, inevitable collapse, responsibility, or a duty to make sacrifices.</td></tr><tr><td>Permitted inferences (<code>allowed_inference</code>)</td><td>1. Candidate overload, insufficient margins, maintenance gaps, and differences in recovery</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited leaps (<code>prohibited_leap</code>)</td><td>1. Assuming people taking up the work should continue to bear it<br>2. Treating high load as proof of devotion<br>3. Equating a subject under overload with a mechanism of destabilization</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>C. Nine-Axis Scale and Object Contract</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nine-axis scale profile (<code>scale_profile</code>)</td><td>SP=&lt;A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J&gt;. A=Aggregation level covers individual task loads, cases of uptake, populations of positions or groups, distributions of load and capacity, and aggregation rules. X=Spatial extent covers sites of work or care, organizational boundaries, digital workspaces, and the extent of outsourcing across domains. T=Time span covers instantaneous peaks, persistent backlogs, recovery lags, and windows across periods and generations. O=Organizational level covers uptake roles, teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. C=Causal level covers task events, mechanisms of interaction between tasks and capacity, meso-level bottleneck structures, institutional allocation, and systemic conditions. R=Resolution covers original task and working-hour records, load sequences, cases of uptake, capacity distributions, delay and error metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. I=Scope of influence covers people directly taking up the work, people dependent on services, indirect substitutes, second-order spillovers, and costs across domains and generations. N=Network topology scope covers task dependencies, key bottlenecks, substitute nodes, recovery paths, and outsourcing networks across domains. J=Scope of jurisdiction and authorization requires separate authorization records for task assignment, resource adjustments, stopping, use of performance evaluations, and remedy. Preserve the distributions of load and capacity</td></tr><tr><td>Effective object (<code>effective_object</code>)</td><td>The structural relation of tasks and coordination requirements to available capacity and recovery margins within a given window</td></tr><tr><td>Scale invariants (<code>scale_invariants</code>)</td><td>1. Load, capacity, recovery, and positions bearing costs</td></tr><tr><td>Required additions for scale elevation (<code>required_scale_additions</code>)</td><td>1. Load distribution<br>2. Masking by aggregation<br>3. Carryover of responsibility<br>4. Intergenerational impacts</td></tr><tr><td>What changes with scale (<code>changing_semantics</code>)</td><td>1. Bottlenecks, capacity, and ways of recovering can change with the level</td></tr><tr><td>Inapplicable objects (<code>non_applicable_objects</code>)</td><td>1. Processes without sustained disequilibrium, maintenance requirements, or human uptake requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited elevations (<code>forbidden_elevation</code>)</td><td>1. Letting average load mask local overload</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>D. State, Evidence, and Variable Flows</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State set (<code>state</code>)</td><td>1. Low load<br>2. Bearable<br>3. Critical<br>4. Overloaded<br>5. Recovering</td></tr><tr><td>Observables (<code>observables</code>)</td><td>1. Task volume per unit of time, backlog, delays, and error rate<br>2. Capacity of personnel and resources, hidden labor, and availability of substitutes<br>3. Stopping, absence, exit, and recovery curves<br>4. Differences between average load and its distribution at key local uptake positions</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence requirements (<code>evidence</code>)</td><td>1. Task volume<br>2. Delays and errors<br>3. Personnel and resources<br>4. Recovery records<br>5. Exit and absence<br>6. For the stated objective, distinguish execution, simultaneous activities, management, being on call, and opportunity costs. Specify overlaps, units, and transfers across positions. Do not sum them directly as a single burden<br>7. When the task in question depends on continuous blocks of time, also record how responsibilities as the default contact, being on call, and filling in at short notice cut into the usable whole blocks. Do not convert this into hours</td></tr><tr><td>Input dependencies and interface content (<code>input_dependencies</code>)</td><td>1. Uptake layer<br>2. Drive–uptake chain<br>3. Conditional potential field<br>4. R0 registers task volume, capacity, backlog, and recovery for the same type, time window, and position bearing the load. It does not require G2 as a premise for description.<br>5. R1 uses G2 and CM-LOAD for channel effects. R3 checks the current state, its formation, and effects across windows under §4.5 for accumulation through carriers. R2 tests the additional historical conditional increment separately under G3. H5 traces cannot substitute for evidence of later effects or testing under G3.<br>6. Lack of formal mechanism support for a route does not cancel findings of general harm, continuing burdens, or historical causation already supported by independent domain evidence. Register conclusions separately according to their own grounds.</td></tr><tr><td>Output effects and variable flows (<code>output_effects</code>)</td><td>1. State updates<br>2. Behavior of destabilization<br>3. Maintenance and repair needs</td></tr><tr><td>Time window and lag (<code>time_window_and_lag</code>)</td><td>Distinguish instantaneous peaks, persistent backlogs, recovery lags, and intergenerational costs</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty (<code>uncertainty</code>)</td><td>Record hidden labor, outsourced costs, and absence for reasons of protection</td></tr><tr><td>Local exclusion zone (<code>local_exclusion_zone</code>)</td><td>Informal labor, family care, outsourcing, and uptake positions with low visibility</td></tr><tr><td>Affected positions (<code>affected_positions</code>)</td><td>1. People taking up the work<br>2. People dependent on services<br>3. People taking over<br>4. Positions to which costs spill over</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>E. Uptake, Responsibility, Normative Status, Ceilings, and Correction</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Uptake carriers (<code>carrier</code>)</td><td>1. Personnel<br>2. Positions<br>3. Procedures<br>4. Facilities<br>5. Budgets</td></tr><tr><td>Responsible parties (<code>responsible_subject</code>)</td><td>1. Task assigners<br>2. Resource allocators<br>3. Parties granting authorization<br>4. Parties responsible for oversight<br>5. Parties responsible for remedy</td></tr><tr><td>Normative status (<code>normative_status</code>)</td><td>High efficiency or the ability to carry a heavy load does not establish normative justification</td></tr><tr><td>Judgment ceiling (<code>judgment_ceiling</code>)</td><td>R0 describes tasks, capacity, and recovery within the same window. R1 uses G2 and CM-LOAD to explain a specified channel within stated limits. R3 uses §4.5 to judge cumulative damage or delayed recovery through transmission by a current carrier. R2 judges historical increments beyond the current representation only with additional G3. Persistent correlation, a temporary overload, or an overall average cannot substitute for the corresponding evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>Action ceiling (<code>action_ceiling</code>)</td><td>This variable produces only descriptions of load, capacity, recovery, local overload, and needs to reduce load or add resources. It does not authorize task reduction, resource commitments, personnel action based on performance, or compelling people to bear the load. Any real-world adjustment separately requires C12, explicit N premises at runtime, J authorization, and O procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Counterexamples (<code>counterexamples</code>)</td><td>1. Average capacity overall is adequate, but some key uptake positions remain overloaded<br>2. Metaphors of entropy or resilience alone fail to identify tasks, capacity, or recovery channels</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal (<code>appeal</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, people taking up work can report hidden labor, overload, and recovery needs through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection. This triggers review independent of the original judgment about load or the chain of performance or task decisions</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback (<code>rollback</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, once rollback is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline revoke erroneous load judgments and their effects on performance or responsibility and actually restore task, resource, and record states. Preserve versions and verification of completion</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"A.9"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=a(d,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,y as default};
